{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787077864,"nonce":"hib3gR0j2qXMINKYGgagKhgqj3w=","title":"**What is wrong.** `alert_groups` is a stored row whose entire identity is a pure function of four","message":"labels, and the four are welded into Go. `db/migrations/00050_derived_group_key.sql:143` states it as\nschema documentation: `group_key` is *\"gk_ + 26 base32hex chars over sha256(org_id, cluster_key,\ncanon(alertname, namespace-or-empty)) (SPEC §C.4, ADR 0038). FIXED, NOT CONFIGURABLE: a tunable split\nkey reinvents group_by inside oto and re-inherits the problem it was built to escape.\"* So the table\nholds no fact an operator supplied and no fact upstream sent — it holds a **decision oto made**, and\nthe decision is the same one for every org, every cluster and every alertname forever.\n\nCONTEXT.md §3:85 says what that decision is FOR in the same breath as it names the object: an\nAlertGroup *\"decides which facts share a message\"* and *\"Owns exactly one Slack thread.\"* Deciding\nwhich facts share a message is the definition of a notification policy. oto has a\n`notification_policies` table for exactly that layer, and this decision is not in it: `00011_notification.sql`'s\n`notification_policies` carries `matchers`, `reasons`, `channel_ids`, `throttle`, the reminder and\n(since `00058`) the digest window and floor — and **no** `group_by` and **no** subject-kind column.\n\nThe consequence is not abstract. Because `node` is not one of the four axes, **two alerts that share a\n`node` label but differ on `alertname` or on `namespace` cannot be collapsed into one conversation by\nany configuration oto exposes** — two pods reporting disk pressure from different namespaces on the\nsame node are two groups and two Slack threads, and there is no knob. It is worse than \"not\nconfigurable\": a policy cannot even *see* `node`. `Policy.Matches`\n(`internal/notification/domain/policy.go:293`) is fed `snap.Group.GroupLabels` at every call site —\n`internal/notification/service/notify.go:291`, `digest.go:255`, `reminder.go:127`, `view.go:593` — and\nsince ADR 0038 those labels are `SplitLabels`, i.e. `alertname` plus at most `namespace`\n(`internal/grouping/domain/group.go:528-530`). `00050:146` says so in the column comment: *\"EVERY\nNOTIFICATION MATCHER IS FED THIS MAP.\"*\n\n**The demonstration.** `digest` already does grouping-as-policy, and it does it with **no group row at\nall**. `db/migrations/00058_a_digest_is_a_window_over_a_namespace.sql` gives a digest\n`subject_kind = 'digest'`, ties `subject_id` to `policy_id` (`notifications_subject_ck`, `:319-320`:\n`subject_kind = 'digest' AND digest_window_start IS NOT NULL AND (policy_id IS NULL OR subject_id =\npolicy_id)`), drops `NOT NULL` from `notifications.group_id`, and keys the conversation\n`(channel_id, 'digest', policy_id)` — `:124`, *\"a digest thread is `(channel_id, 'digest', policy_id)`\"*\n— one thread per policy per channel under the unchanged\n`threads_subject_uniq UNIQUE (channel_id, subject_kind, subject_id)`. A grouping decision made by a\npolicy, addressed by the policy's id, owning its own conversation. It works, it shipped, and it needed\nno entity.\n\nThree more things say the design already knows:\n\n*(a) The table now carries TWO competing conversation keys, discriminated by a CHECK.* `00058` adds\n`notifications_target_ck CHECK (subject_kind = 'digest' OR group_id IS NOT NULL)`. That is one arm per\nkey: policy-keyed, or group-keyed. Every future collapse kind is another arm and another migration.\n\n*(b) The exception is in Go too, not only in SQL.* `threadSubjectOf`\n(`internal/notification/service/notify.go:702`, called at `:659`) is already two-armed — `digest`\nreturns `(SubjectDigest, n.SubjectID)`, everything else returns `(SubjectAlertGroup, n.GroupID)` — and\nits own comment says why the second arm exists: *\"A conversation is keyed per GROUP; only the FACT\nvaries.\"* The pin is a pin, and the digest arm is the hole in it.\n\n*(c) `group_id` needs prose to say what it is.* `00056_a_notification_says_what_it_is_about.sql:138`\ncomments the column *\"THE DELIVERY TARGET, not the subject\"*, and `:158` comments\n`channel_threads.subject_kind` with the finding this ticket is built on: *\"thread identity is a POLICY\ndecision and was welded to the alert grouping by a one-line CHECK.\"* `00056` widened the CHECK and left\nthe welding.\n\nThere is precedent for deriving rather than storing, on this exact object.\n`db/migrations/00051_membership_is_derived_not_recorded.sql:103` drops `alert_group_members` outright\nand `:100` re-comments the surviving column as *\"THE MEMBERSHIP … and the only record of it since\nalert_group_members was dropped.\"*\n\nAnd the render half of the table is already derivable. `alert_groups` has **26 columns today** — 27 in\n`00008_grouping.sql`, plus `synthetic` (`00039_delivery_drills.sql:74`), minus `storm_mode` and\n`storm_since` (`00059_no_column_remembers_a_storm.sql:100-101`). **Eleven of the 26 are a cache over\n`alert_cases`**: the six counts (`firing_count`, `suppressed_count`, `resolved_count`, `expired_count`,\n`total_count`, `acked_count`), `severity` (the comment says \"max member severity\"), `title` (which\n`internal/grouping/domain/group.go:534` records is now *the alertname alone* — \"SINCE ADR 0038 THE\nLEFTOVER SET IS EMPTY BY CONSTRUCTION\", so the two branches computing it are unreachable), and the\nthree timestamps `first_seen_at`, `last_activity_at`, `closed_at`.\n\n**Why it matters.** oto's product claim is that it decides which facts share a message better than\nAlertmanager does. That decision is currently unreachable to the operator, unreachable to a policy,\nand stored as a row that must be opened, closed, generation-bumped, counted and swept. The cost lands\nin three places: an operator who wants one thread per node cannot have it and gets no error saying so;\n`notifications` grows a CHECK arm per collapse kind; and `channel_threads.subject_kind` keeps a comment\nadmitting the welding instead of a schema without it.\n\n**Where.** The decision and its rules: `db/migrations/00050_derived_group_key.sql:143,146` ·\n`docs/design/SPEC.md:723` (§C.4) · `docs/adr/0038-the-group-key-is-derived-from-the-alerts-own-labels.md:41-43,49,117`\n· `CONTEXT.md:85,92` (§3). The entity: `db/migrations/00008_grouping.sql` (the 27-column `CREATE TABLE`)\n· `internal/grouping/**` (sole writer `repository/group.go`). The notification seam:\n`db/migrations/00056_a_notification_says_what_it_is_about.sql:138,158` ·\n`db/migrations/00058_a_digest_is_a_window_over_a_namespace.sql:124,273,275-276,319-320` ·\n`db/migrations/00011_notification.sql` (`notification_policies`, `channel_threads` — neither has\n`group_by` nor `generation`) · `internal/notification/service/notify.go:291,659,702` ·\n`internal/notification/domain/policy.go:293`. The membership precedent:\n`db/migrations/00051_membership_is_derived_not_recorded.sql:100,103`. The surfaces:\n`internal/grouping/api/{handlers,router,dto}.go` (nine endpoints) · `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:1990,2109,2141,2173,2246,2298,2361,2414,2478`\n· `web/src/App.tsx:163-176` · `web/src/components/AppShell.tsx:223-229` ·\n`internal/notification/service/view.go:489` · `internal/alerts/domain/event.go:99-119` ·\n`test/arch/sqltables_test.go:151,176,359,536`.\n\n**Blast radius, measured on this branch rather than copied.** `git grep -o -E\n\"alert_groups|AlertGroups|AlertGroup|alert-groups|alert_group\"` returns **961 occurrences across 175\nfiles**, of which **139 are non-test**. By area: `internal/grouping` 153 lines, `docs` 119,\n`docs-site` 111 (the generated mirror), `web/src` 109, `db` 106, `internal/notification` 83, `test` 81,\n`internal/alerts` 53, `api` 36, then `internal/drill` and `internal/channels` at 13 each.\n`api/openapi/openapi.yaml` carries **38 sites** across **9 `/api/v1/alert-groups*` paths**\n(`\"\"`, `/{id}`, `/{id}/alerts`, `/{id}/timeline`, `/{id}/ack`, `/{id}/unack`, `/{id}/comments`,\n`/{id}/snooze`, `/{id}/unsnooze`) — so the generated client moves too.\n`test/arch/sqltables_test.go` **is** affected, at four sites: `:151` (the ownership row\n`table: \"alert_groups\", owner: \"grouping\", access: tableDelete`), `:176`, `:359` (`syntheticGuarded`)\nand `:536`. `test/scope/allocation_test.go` is **NOT** affected — it contains zero occurrences of\n`alert_group`, `AlertGroup` or even the substring `group`. The six `group.*` event literals appear on\n**82 lines across 27 files**.\n\nA prior measurement of \"~445 occurrences across ~124 files and 36 openapi sites\" is **corrected** by\nthe above: 961 / 175 / 38.\n\n**The target design.** Delete `alert_groups`. Drop `alert_cases.group_id`. `group_by` becomes a\n`notification_policies` column and the collapse key is computed at delivery from the alert's own\nlabels, which means policies must be fed the **full** label set rather than `SplitLabels` — that is a\nprerequisite, not a detail, because today `Policy.Matches` cannot see the label a policy would group\nby. `notifications.group_id` becomes a `(conversation_kind, conversation_id)` pair mirroring\n`(subject_kind, subject_id)`, which retires `notifications_target_ck` as a special case and lets a\npolicy-collapse kind slot in without a further migration. `channel_threads` gains `generation` —\nit has none today, and today's generation is smuggled in through `alert_groups.generation` inside\n`subject_id` (`00011`'s comment on the column: *\"alert_groups.id (one generation)\"*). A per-channel\ngeneration is MORE honest than a shared one, because a Slack channel can be archived\n(`threads_deadmap_ck` admits `is_archived`) while Teams lives. Delete the nine `/api/v1/alert-groups*`\nendpoints and the `/groups` UI routes; retarget the Slack deep link at the Case, which `2c26520` made\nthe product's first destination (`web/src/App.tsx:155` redirects `/` to `/cases`). Policies gain a\nsubject-kind binding (alert or case): the subject axis has existed since `00056` and no policy can\nfilter on it. And policies gain count-over-window conditions — \"PodRestart opened 5 cases in 1h\".\n\nThat last one is **the same machinery, not a new concept**: `notification_policies.throttle` is\nalready `{\"max\":N,\"window_s\":S}` (`00011_notification.sql:143`, *\"Per-subject rate cap\"*) and\n`digest_floor` is already *\"the minimum number of Cases that must have OPENED inside the window\"*\n(`internal/notification/domain/digest.go:69-74`). Two policy-level counts over a policy-level window\nalready ship. This adds axes to them.\n\n**Two `group.*` event types, not six.** `internal/alerts/domain/event.go:99-119` declares six, but\n`retiredEventTypes` already holds `group.member_joined`, `group.member_left`, `group.storm_started`\nand `group.storm_ended`. Only `group.opened` and `group.closed` are still appended, and those two are\nwhat this ticket retires — by the `00051` / `00054` bargain (readable, unwritable), never deleted,\nbecause `alert_events` is retained thirteen months.\n\n**This supersedes flap detection, and that retirement has already happened.** `alerts.flap_score` and\n`alerts.is_flapping` are retired in place — `internal/alerts/service/sweep.go:712-745` and\n`internal/alerts/repository/alert.go:1215-1241` record it, and the reason is exactly a count that\nstopped counting: the score was an EWMA over what `stateChangeCountsSQL`\n(`internal/alerts/repository/event.go:534`) counts — `case.opened`, `case.resolved`, `case.expired`,\n`case.suppressed`, `case.unsuppressed` — and the case retention window W (`00057`) damps a flap at case\nformation, so a re-fire inside W appends neither. *\"Six flaps in ten minutes used to append twelve\ncounted events; damped they append about two, against `DefaultFlapThreshold = 5` over\n`DefaultFlapWindow = 7200 s`\"*, so `is_flapping` read FALSE exactly when the alert flapped hardest.\n⚠️ `00057:83-92` still asserts the opposite — *\"`alerts.flap_score` AND `alerts.is_flapping` ARE NOT\nMADE DEAD BY W AND ARE NOT RETIRED HERE\"* — because that header predates the retirement; the\nmigration is historical and is not edited, but a reader who stops there gets the wrong answer. A\npolicy count condition is the correct replacement: operator-configurable per alertname instead of a\nhardcoded threshold of 5 over 7200 s.\n\n**⛔ RECORDED AS REFUSED, so it is not re-litigated: oto does not mint Alerts.** A policy condition\nemits a NOTIFICATION, never a synthetic alert. `alert_key` is an injective hash of the full upstream\nlabel set, and `internal/alerts/domain/labels.go:305-307` states why that is identity rather than a\nkey: *\"The serialisation must be INJECTIVE — two different label sets must never produce one byte\nstring — because it IS alert identity. Two Alerts that collide here are one row, one timeline and one\nSlack thread.\"* A minted alert would therefore need a label set no upstream ever sent, would carry no\n`generator_url`, and would give `rule_fingerprint` — which hashes a rule's labels and annotations\n(`labels.go:301,544,555`) — nothing to hash. That destroys the distinction between oto's opinions and\nthe monitoring system's facts, which is the product.\n\n**⚠️ THIS COLLIDES WITH A SPEC RULING AND AN ADR, AND THE COLLISION IS NOT WAVED THROUGH.**\nSPEC §C.3 (`docs/design/SPEC.md:53`) rules: *\"The configurable grouping-rule engine is CUT … Still\nfixed, still not a rule engine.\"* SPEC §C.4 (`:723`) repeats it: *\"The axes are `(org, cluster,\nalertname, namespace-or-∅)` and they are FIXED, not configurable. A tunable split key reinvents\n`group_by` inside oto and re-inherits the problem it was built to escape.\"* `:733` records that\n`severity`, `pod` and `instance` were each considered and refused as axes. ADR 0038:49 says the same\nand ADR 0038:117 lists *\"Keep AM's grouping, add an oto-side override\"* as a rejected alternative\nbecause *\"a tunable split key is `group_by` again, inside oto, with the same failure mode and a second\nplace to be wrong\"*. The distinction this ticket rests on, which must be written down rather than\nassumed: what §C.3 and §C.4 cut was a configurable **identity** — the key of a stored entity that owns\na thread, computed once at ingest, on which membership and generation depend. What this ticket adds is\na configurable **delivery-time collapse key** owned by a policy, computed per policy per delivery,\nwhich no row's identity depends on. That may be the right distinction; it is not currently anywhere,\nand until it is in §C.4 and in an ADR that supersedes 0038, this ticket reads as reopening a CUT.\n\n**One behaviour change, stated so it is not discovered as a surprise.** Today an `acked` on one pod\nlands in that pod's group thread. Under policy grouping it lands in the collapse thread — so acking\n`pod-a` posts into a conversation titled for `node-7`. That is intended. It also means\n`CONTEXT.md:85` (*\"Owns exactly one Slack thread\"*) and `CONTEXT.md:92` (*\"ChannelThread | The binding\nof an AlertGroup to `(slack_channel_id, root_ts)`\"*) both need rewriting — and `:92` is **already\nfalse** as of `00058`, which binds a digest thread to a policy.\n\n**Note the pre-release window.** `git tag` returns nothing — **zero tags** — and\n`.github/workflows/release.yml:25-30` publishes an image only on `push` of a tag matching\n`v[0-9]+.[0-9]+.[0-9]+*` (`push: true` at `:124`). No `/groups/\u003cid\u003e` link has ever left this\nrepository, so breaking the deep link costs nothing today and costs a permanent redirect after the\nfirst tag.\n\n**Scope.** large\n\n**Done when.**\n1. `alert_groups` is dropped, `alert_cases.group_id` is dropped, and `internal/grouping` either no\n   longer exists or no longer owns a table — `test/arch/sqltables_test.go:151` is the row that has to\n   change, and `:359`'s `syntheticGuarded` and `:536`'s retention list with it.\n2. `notification_policies` carries `group_by`, and the collapse key is computed at delivery. A test\n   asserts two alerts differing on `alertname` and sharing `node` land in ONE conversation under a\n   `group_by: [node]` policy — the case that is impossible today.\n3. Policies are matched against the alert's FULL label set, not `SplitLabels`. Landing (2) without\n   this ships a `group_by` over labels the matcher cannot see.\n4. `notifications` carries `(conversation_kind, conversation_id)` and `notifications_target_ck` is\n   gone as a special case; a digest is one conversation kind among several rather than the exception\n   in a CHECK. `threadSubjectOf` (`internal/notification/service/notify.go:702`) reads the pair off\n   the row instead of branching on `n.Digest()`.\n5. `channel_threads` carries `generation`, included in `threads_subject_uniq`.\n6. `group.opened` and `group.closed` are RETIRED, not deleted — added to `retiredEventTypes`\n   (`internal/alerts/domain/event.go`) beside the four already there, still decodable, refused at the\n   writer. `subject_kind = 'alert_group'` is canonicalised on read in BOTH `notifications` and\n   `channel_threads`, the way `00056` set out.\n7. The nine `/api/v1/alert-groups*` paths and their 38 OpenAPI sites are removed, the generated client\n   is regenerated, and the removal is a declared breaking change. The Slack deep link\n   (`internal/notification/service/view.go:489`) mints a Case URL, and the three `/groups` routes in\n   `web/src/App.tsx:171-176` plus the ⛔ guard at `:163` and the rail note at\n   `web/src/components/AppShell.tsx:223-229` go with them.\n8. Policies carry a subject-kind binding (alert | case) and count-over-window conditions, built as\n   further axes on the machinery `throttle` and `digest_floor` already are, not as a second mechanism.\n9. SPEC §C.3 (`:53`) and §C.4 (`:723,733`) are amended, and an ADR supersedes 0038, stating the\n   identity-versus-delivery-collapse distinction in (2) explicitly. Without this the change reads as\n   reopening a ruling the SPEC records as CUT.\n10. `CONTEXT.md:85` and `:92` are rewritten — `:92` is already false today — and the behaviour change\n    in the ack thread is documented where an operator will meet it, not only here.\n11. Ticket `23303a0` is closed as superseded: the table is not renamed, it is deleted.","files":null}]}